  "id": "Compound::DB00207 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Azithromycin is linked to Cyp3a4 through a series of molecular interactions that involve macrolide antibiotics. The evidence suggests that Azithromycin, as a member of the macrolide class, binds to Cyp3a4, which is a key enzyme in the cytochrome P450 system. This relationship is supported by multiple paths, all of which converge on the shared classification of Azithromycin and macrolides, followed by the binding of macrolides to Cyp3a4. The lowest common ancestors across these paths reinforce the central role of macrolides in mediating the interaction between Azithromycin and Cyp3a4. The mechanism appears to be consistent, with Azithromycin acting as a macrolide that, through its structural similarity to other macrolides like clarithromycin and erythromycin, facilitates the binding to Cyp3a4. This pathway highlights the importance of macrolide antibiotics in modulating Cyp3a4 activity, potentially influencing drug metabolism and therapeutic outcomes.",
